<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Skagerrak</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Skagerrak is a strait between southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a> and the Baltic. It has long been a major shipping route and fishing ground, and the coastal districts of Agder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telemark">Telemark</a> and Vestfold face it. In 1916 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jutland">Battle of Jutland</a>, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as the Battle of the Skagerrak, was fought off its entrance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/skagerrak.jpg" alt="Skagerrak" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Attilios at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carte_Skagerrak-Kattegat2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Skagerrak is a strait about 240 km long and between 80 and 140 km wide. It runs between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, and connects the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>. It deepens toward the Norwegian coast, reaching over 700 metres in the Norwegian Trench. Among its ports are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, Larvik and Kristiansand in Norway, Skagen, Hirtshals and Hanstholm in Denmark, and Uddevalla, Lysekil and Strömstad in Sweden. The International Hydrographic Organization draws its western limit from Hanstholm to the Naze at Lindesnes and its south-eastern limit from Skagen to Paternosterskären.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name most likely means the Skagen strait, after Skagen, a town near the northern cape of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> known as the Skaw. The second part, rak, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> word for a straight waterway, and the name was probably given by Dutch seafarers, as was that of the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>. Older names for the combined Skagerrak and Kattegat were the Norwegian Sea or the Jutland Sea, the latter appearing in the Knýtlinga saga.</p><p>Until the Eider Canal was built in 1784, the Skagerrak was the only way in and out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, and for that reason the strait has carried busy international traffic for centuries. Traffic increased after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>. In 1862 a shortcut, the Thyborøn Channel at the Limfjord in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, was constructed through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> coast, linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> directly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kattegat">Kattegat</a>.</p><p>The strait was strategically important to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in both world wars. The largest naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-jutland">Battle of Jutland</a>, which in Germany is known as the Battle of the Skagerrak, was fought here from 31 May to 1 June 1916. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the importance of controlling the only sea access to the Baltic was a motive for the German invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and for the construction of the northern parts of the Atlantic Wall. Both wars left numerous shipwrecks on the seabed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Skagerrak supports an intensive fishing industry and is home to about 2,000 marine species, among them the Skagerrak cod, a variety of Atlantic cod that spawns off the Norwegian coast. Cold-water coral reefs grow in it, and the Tisler Reef in Ytre Hvaler National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> is the largest known coral reef in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Ytre Hvaler National Park in Norway and Kosterhavet National Park in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> protect parts of the strait. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothenburg">Gothenburg</a> are the only large cities in the region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Skagerrak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Agder faced the <b>Skagerrak</b> and Denmark and lived by farming, fishing and sea trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/agder">Agder</a></li><li><blockquote>The district looked to the sea and the <b>Skagerrak</b>, and its people farmed, fished and traded.</blockquote><a href="/state/grenland">Grenland</a></li></ul></section>
